=== layout: section

# Controversial Biotech and Pharma Ethics

=== layout: subsection

# 40. Controversial Biotechnology

=== layout: bookend

# 40. Questions

@body

- Choose two biotechnology controversies the class has yet to discuss. What concerned people at the time? Whose views changed as evidence or experience accumulated?

=== layout: one-panel

# 40. Choose Two Cases and Compare Them

@body

- Name the technology and what it claims to do.
- Identify what was actually demonstrated and what people disagreed about.
- Ask whether the disagreement is about evidence, values, or who gets to decide.

@citation

[UNESCO, Universal Declaration on Bioethics and Human Rights](https://www.unesco.org/en/legal-affairs/universal-declaration-bioethics-and-human-rights)

=== layout: two-panels

# 40. A Common Format for Comparison

@left

*Case 1*

Who receives the benefit? Who bears the risk? Name one finding that would change your position.

@right

*Case 2*

Ask the same questions. Which disagreement between the two cases is about values?

@citation

[UNESCO, Universal Declaration on Bioethics and Human Rights](https://www.unesco.org/en/legal-affairs/universal-declaration-bioethics-and-human-rights)

=== layout: one-panel

# 40. Could Your Neighbor Buy Naloxone?

@body

In 2023 the FDA approved a naloxone nasal spray for sale without a prescription. Naloxone can reverse an opioid overdose.

- The regulatory change removed one barrier to obtaining this product.
- If a store never stocks it or the price is too high, what benefit will a neighbor actually see?

@citation

[FDA, First over-the-counter naloxone nasal spray (2023)](https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-approves-first-over-counter-naloxone-nasal-spray)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Annual U.S. retail pharmacy naloxone dispensing rates](assets/set7_pharma_evidence/naloxone_dispensing.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 40: Naloxone at the pharmacy\
Through 2016 the series counts prescriptions prepared. From 2017 it counts pickups.

@citation

[CDC, Retail naloxone dispensing (2019), table 1](https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/68/wr/pdfs/mm6831e1-h.pdf)

@speaker-notes

The last two points count prescriptions sold. The earlier five include prescriptions prepared but never picked up. Ask when the climb began. This entire series predates the 2023 over-the-counter approval.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![2018 county naloxone prescriptions per 100,000 residents](assets/set7_pharma_evidence/county_population.webp)

@caption

Topic 40: Naloxone prescriptions per 100,000 residents, 2018\
Find two neighboring counties with different rates. White means no data.

@citation

[CDC, Retail naloxone dispensing (2019), p. 683](https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/68/wr/pdfs/mm6831e1-h.pdf)

@speaker-notes

Start in the Midwest. A white county has missing data. A light blue county has a measured rate in the lowest group. County population is the denominator.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![2018 county naloxone prescriptions per 100 high-dose opioid prescriptions](assets/set7_pharma_evidence/county_prescriptions.webp)

@caption

Topic 40: Naloxone per 100 high-dose opioid prescriptions, 2018\
Can either map tell you how many patients got both drugs?

@citation

[CDC, Retail naloxone dispensing (2019), p. 683](https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/68/wr/pdfs/mm6831e1-h.pdf)

@speaker-notes

No. These are two totals from the same county. The map does not link a naloxone prescription to an individual who filled an opioid prescription. A county can change color when we change the denominator.

=== layout: two-panels

# 40. Who can use the HeLa genome?

@left

![Members of Henrietta Lacks's family at a Virginia historical marker dedicated to her in 2011](assets/set7_people_late/lacks_family.jpg)

@right

Henrietta Lacks's family gathered at this Virginia marker in 2011. Two years later, family members and NIH reached an agreement about access to HeLa genome data.

A researcher wants to download those data. What should the family get to know about the project?

@citation

[Photo: Lacks family/Virginia Department of Historic Resources, via Nature](https://doi.org/10.1038/500141a)  |  [NIH-Lacks Family Agreement](https://grants.nih.gov/policy-and-compliance/policy-topics/sharing-policies/dms/privacy/nih-lacks-family-agreement)

@speaker-notes

Compare access to genome data with access to naloxone on the preceding slides. Students choose two other controversies for their own talking points.

=== layout: one-panel

# 40. A 60-second Position You Could Defend

@body

Suppose a rural clinic can join a tissue bank, but the community has no seat on its review board.

- A resident might support sharing samples if local patients receive results they can use.
- Another resident might require a voice in deciding future uses. What evidence could resolve the first issue? What agreement could resolve the second?

@citation

[UNESCO, Universal Declaration on Bioethics and Human Rights](https://www.unesco.org/en/legal-affairs/universal-declaration-bioethics-and-human-rights)

=== layout: subsection

# 41. Other Pharmaceutical Ethics

=== layout: bookend

# 41. Questions

@body

- Select two other topics in pharmaceutical regulation or ethics that have not been discussed before in class and present them.

=== layout: one-panel

# 41. Pricing and Evidence Disclosure

@body

- A drug price can affect patient access even when a treatment works.
- Selective reporting can make a treatment look more useful or safer than the full evidence suggests.
- Distinguish a question about affordability from a question about reliable evidence.

@citation

[Turner et al., Selective publication of antidepressant trials (2008)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18199864/)  |  [KFF Health Tracking Poll, March 2026, question 7](https://files.kff.org/attachment/topline-kff-health-tracking-poll-march-2026.pdf)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Generic manufacturer price ratios by number of producers](assets/set7_pharma_evidence/generic_prices.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 41: The brand price before generic entry is 100%\
Dots show median price ratios. Bars span the middle 50%. What would a patient pay?

@citation

[FDA, Generic competition and drug prices (2019), p. 9](https://www.fda.gov/media/133509/download)

@speaker-notes

The FDA followed 181 products with initial generic entry in 2015-2017. With one producer, the median manufacturer price was about 61% of the earlier brand price. With four it was about 21%. Patients can pay a different amount at the pharmacy.

=== layout: two-panels

# 41. Why did Frances Kelsey ask for more evidence?

@left

FDA reviewer Frances Oldham Kelsey kept asking the company selling thalidomide for better safety data. The drug was already on sale overseas.

President Kennedy honored her in 1962. What would you have wanted the company to show her before the drug went on sale here?

@right

![Frances Oldham Kelsey receives a public service award from President John F. Kennedy in 1962](assets/set7_people_late/frances_kelsey.jpg)

@citation

[FDA, Frances Oldham Kelsey](https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history-exhibits/frances-oldham-kelsey-medical-reviewer-famous-averting-public-health-tragedy)  |  [Photo: FDA History](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FDA_History_-_Dr._Kelsey_and_John_F._Kennedy.jpg)

=== layout: two-panels

# 41. Which antidepressant trials reached readers?

@left

![Psychiatrist Erick Turner](assets/set7_people_late/erick_turner.jpg)

@right

Erick Turner and his colleagues compared the FDA's files on 74 antidepressant studies with the medical journals. They found that 31% of the studies went unpublished.

If you read only the journals, how would you know a study was missing?

@citation

[Turner et al., Selective publication (2008)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18199864/)  |  [Portrait: OHSU](https://news.ohsu.edu/2026/07/20/accolades-awards-honors-and-appointments-july-6926159)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![How Does the Record Change the Picture?](assets/set7_drawings/publication_bias.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 41: Which Record Did the Reader See?\
How did selective publication change the apparent share of positive trials?

@citation

[Turner et al., Selective publication of antidepressant trials (2008)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18199864/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 41. When an Effective Medicine Stays at the Pharmacy

@body

In KFF's March 2026 U.S. poll, adults reported what they did in the past year because of cost.

- *27%* left a prescription unfilled. *19%* cut pills in half or skipped doses.
- One person could report both. Why would adding the percentages give a misleading estimate of how many people had trouble affording medicine?

@citation

[KFF Health Tracking Poll, March 2026, question 7](https://files.kff.org/attachment/topline-kff-health-tracking-poll-march-2026.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 41. What Outcome Was Promised at the Start?

@body

Chan and colleagues compared clinical trial protocols with the papers that followed them. They found differences in which outcomes were reported.

- An outcome specified before results are known gives readers a reference point.
- If a paper highlights a favorable secondary outcome, where would you look for the original primary outcome?

@citation

[Chan et al., Protocols compared with published trials (2004)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15161896/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 41. An ad says the drug cuts the risk in half

@body

Suppose a company advertises a drug that prevents headaches. Its trial followed two groups for one week.

{table-style=standard}
| Group | People in trial | Had a headache |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Placebo | 100 | 10 |
| Drug | 100 | 5 |

Would you let the company run that ad? Write one sentence you would want to see beside the headline.

@citation

Class example with invented counts.  |  [FDA, Communicating benefits and risks in drug ads (2023)](https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/presenting-quantitative-efficacy-and-risk-information-direct-consumer-dtc-promotional-labeling-and)

@speaker-notes

Give the class a moment to write a line in chat. Ask which counts they used.

=== layout: two-panels

# 41. How much did the drug help?

@left

*The headline*

The risk fell from 10 in 100 to 5 in 100. Five is half of ten. That's a 50% relative reduction.

@right

*What a patient might ask*

For every 100 people in these groups, the drug group had 5 fewer people with a headache that week.

@citation

Class example with invented counts.  |  [FDA, Communicating benefits and risks in drug ads (2023)](https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/presenting-quantitative-efficacy-and-risk-information-direct-consumer-dtc-promotional-labeling-and)

@speaker-notes

Ask for one more fact a patient would want before deciding: side effects, headache severity, or what happens after the first week.
